# Leaf parsers

The combinators in `parser.lavin` join parsers; the parsers here read text. They are the words a
grammar is spelled with: literal text, characters, names, numbers, white space and quoted strings.
Each is written directly against the input, moving its offset itself, rather than built from
smaller parsers. They run for every character of every text, so they should cost little, and
reading a number one character-parser at a time would allocate a value per digit.

    module dev.avinlang.parsing

## Characters

Letters and white space outside ASCII are a Unicode question, and the answer comes from the system
library rather than from a table kept here. These two declarations have no body: `@sysl` binds each
to the system library's function of that name.

    @sysl("sysl.unicode.is_alpha")
    private isUnicodeLetter(c: char) -> bool

    @sysl("sysl.unicode.is_space")
    private isUnicodeSpace(c: char) -> bool

The input is UTF-8, so moving past a character means moving past the bytes that encode it, between
one and four.

    // The number of bytes UTF-8 encodes `c` in.
    private width(c: char) -> int
        val code = int(c)
        if code < 0x80 then 1 elif code < 0x800 then 2 elif code < 0x10000 then 3 else 4

The character tests are public, for a grammar's own `satisfy` and `takeWhile`. Most text is ASCII,
so each answers ASCII itself and asks Unicode only about the rest. `(code | 0x20)` lowercases an
ASCII letter, so one range check covers both cases.

    // Whether `c` is an ASCII decimal digit.
    isDigit(c: char) -> bool = c >= '0' && c <= '9'

    // Whether `c` is a letter, in any script.
    isLetter(c: char) -> bool
        val code = int(c)
        if code < 0x80 then (code | 0x20) >= 0x61 && (code | 0x20) <= 0x7a else isUnicodeLetter(c)

    // Whether `c` is white space, in any script.
    isSpace(c: char) -> bool
        val code = int(c)
        if code < 0x80 then code == 0x20 || (code >= 0x09 && code <= 0x0d) else isUnicodeSpace(c)

A name is what most programming languages call an identifier: a letter or `_` to begin, then
letters, digits and `_`.

    // Whether `c` may begin a name: a letter or `_`.
    private startsName(c: char) -> bool = c == '_' || isLetter(c)

    // Whether `c` may continue a name: a letter, a digit or `_`.
    private continuesName(c: char) -> bool = c == '_' || isDigit(c) || isLetter(c)

## Helpers for reading

Numbers and strings are made of ASCII punctuation and digits, which can be tested a byte at a time
without decoding a character. `byteIs` does that, and is false at the end of the text rather than
trapping.

    // Whether the byte at `at` is the ASCII character `b`.
    private byteIs(input: Input, at: int, b: int) -> bool = at < input.length && int(input.text.byteAt(at)) == b

    // Moves past the characters `p` accepts.
    private skip(input: Input, p: char -> bool)
        while !input.atEnd()
            val c = input.current()
            if !p(c) then return
            input.offset += width(c)

    // Moves past the ASCII digits.
    private skipDigits(input: Input)
        while input.offset < input.length
            val b = int(input.text.byteAt(input.offset))
            if b < 0x30 || b > 0x39 then return
            input.offset += 1

## Literal text

`lit` reads exactly the text it is given. What it expects is the text in backquotes, worked out once
when the parser is made rather than on every failure, so an error reads ``expected `]` ``.

    // Exactly `text`.
    lit(text: string) -> Parser[string]
        val what = s"`$text`"
        val n = text.byteLength()
        Parser(input ->
            if input.text.startsWith(text, input.offset)
                input.offset += n
                Some(text)
            else input.fail(what))

    // Exactly the character `c`.
    lit(c: char) -> Parser[char]
        val what = s"`$c`"
        val n = width(c)
        Parser(input ->
            if !input.atEnd() && input.current() == c
                input.offset += n
                Some(c)
            else input.fail(what))

## Characters by a test

`satisfy` reads one character a test accepts. A test has no name to put in an error, so the
grammar gives one: `satisfy("vowel", c -> "aeiou".contains(s"$c"))`.

    // One character `p` accepts, described as `what` when there is none.
    satisfy(what: string, p: char -> bool) -> Parser[char] = Parser(input ->
        if input.atEnd() then return input.fail(what)
        val c = input.current()
        if !p(c) then return input.fail(what)
        input.offset += width(c)
        Some(c))

`takeWhile` reads a run of such characters and answers it as one slice of the text, with no
character-by-character values in between. `takeWhile` accepts an empty run; `takeWhile1` needs one
character at least, and so needs a name for its error.

    // The characters `p` accepts, as many as there are, none included.
    takeWhile(p: char -> bool) -> Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        val start = input.offset
        skip(input, p)
        Some(input.text.slice(start, input.offset)))

    // The characters `p` accepts, at least one, described as `what` when there is none.
    takeWhile1(what: string, p: char -> bool) -> Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        val start = input.offset
        skip(input, p)
        if input.offset == start then input.fail(what) else Some(input.text.slice(start, input.offset)))

## Digits, names, white space and the end

These are values rather than functions, since they take nothing, and `lazy` so that each is made
once, the first time it is used.

    // One ASCII decimal digit or more.
    lazy val digits: Parser[string] = takeWhile1("digit", isDigit)

    // A name: a letter or `_`, then letters, digits and `_`.
    lazy val ident: Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        if input.atEnd() || !startsName(input.current()) then return input.fail("identifier")
        val start = input.offset
        skip(input, continuesName)
        Some(input.text.slice(start, input.offset)))

    // White space, none included.
    lazy val whitespace: Parser[unit] = Parser(input ->
        skip(input, isSpace)
        Some(()))

    // The end of the text.
    lazy val eof: Parser[unit] = Parser(input -> if input.atEnd() then Some(()) else input.fail("end of input"))

## Tokens

Most grammars allow white space between any two tokens. The convention that keeps that out of every
rule is to skip white space **after** each token, never before: then the only place a grammar has to
mention it is the very start of the text, `whitespace ~> expr`. `token(p)` is `p` followed by the
white space after it, and `symbol` is the common case of a literal token.

    // `p`, then the white space after it.
    token[T](p: -> Parser[T]) -> Parser[T]
        val q = later(p)
        Parser(input ->
            val r = q.run(input)
            if r is Some(_) then skip(input, isSpace)
            r)

    // Exactly `text`, then the white space after it.
    symbol(text: string) -> Parser[string] = token(lit(text))

## Numbers

An integer is an optional `-` and digits. The digits are found first and converted once, as a
slice of the text, so an integer costs one conversion however long it is. A run of digits that does
not fit a `long` is still an integer to read, so it fails with an expectation that says why, rather
than with `integer`, which would be puzzling beside a perfectly good row of digits.

    // A decimal integer that fits a `long`, with an optional leading `-`.
    lazy val integer: Parser[long] = Parser(input ->
        val start = input.offset
        if byteIs(input, start, 0x2d) then input.offset += 1
        val first = input.offset
        skipDigits(input)
        if input.offset == first
            input.offset = start
            return input.fail("integer")
        input.text.slice(start, input.offset).toLong() match
            Some(n) -> Some(n)
            None ->
                input.offset = start
                input.fail("integer that fits a long"))

A decimal is a number as JSON writes it: `-`, digits, an optional fraction and an optional exponent.
A fraction or exponent is part of the number only when digits follow it, so `1.` reads as `1` and
leaves the dot for whatever comes next, a method call or the end of a sentence. Where a part turns
out to have no digits, the offset goes back to where that part began.

    // A decimal number: an optional `-`, digits, an optional fraction `.` and digits, and an optional
    // exponent `e` or `E`, a sign and digits, as JSON writes numbers. A `.` or an `e` not followed by
    // digits is not part of it.
    lazy val decimal: Parser[real] = Parser(input ->
        val start = input.offset
        if byteIs(input, start, 0x2d) then input.offset += 1
        val first = input.offset
        skipDigits(input)
        if input.offset == first
            input.offset = start
            return input.fail("number")
        if byteIs(input, input.offset, 0x2e)
            val dot = input.offset
            input.offset += 1
            skipDigits(input)
            if input.offset == dot + 1 then input.offset = dot
        if byteIs(input, input.offset, 0x65) || byteIs(input, input.offset, 0x45)
            val e = input.offset
            input.offset += 1
            if byteIs(input, input.offset, 0x2b) || byteIs(input, input.offset, 0x2d) then input.offset += 1
            val exponent = input.offset
            skipDigits(input)
            if input.offset == exponent then input.offset = e
        input.text.slice(start, input.offset).toReal() match
            Some(x) -> Some(x)
            None ->
                input.offset = start
                input.fail("finite number"))

## Strings

`stringLiteral` reads a string in double quotes with JSON's escapes and answers the text the string
stands for. Most strings in most texts have no escapes at all, so the parser avoids building
anything until it meets one. It notes where the current unescaped **chunk** begins and moves on a
byte at a time; a string with no `\` is answered as one slice of the input. The first `\` makes a
writer, and from then on each chunk is written to it, followed by the character its escape stands
for.

A control character must be escaped in JSON, so a raw newline inside quotes is an error, reported as
the closing `"` that was wanted before it.

    // A string in double quotes, with JSON's escapes: `\"`, `\\`, `\/`, `\b`, `\f`, `\n`, `\r`, `\t` and
    // `\u` with four hexadecimal digits, a pair of them for a character beyond the first 65,536. A
    // control character must be escaped. The value is the text the string stands for.
    lazy val stringLiteral: Parser[string] = Parser(input ->
        if !byteIs(input, input.offset, 0x22) then return input.fail("string")
        input.offset += 1
        var chunk = input.offset
        var out: Option[StringWriter] = None
        loop
            if input.atEnd() then return input.fail("`\"`")
            val b = int(input.text.byteAt(input.offset))
            if b == 0x22
                val last = input.text.slice(chunk, input.offset)
                input.offset += 1
                return out match
                    None -> Some(last)
                    Some(w) ->
                        w.write(last)
                        Some(w.text())
            if b < 0x20 then return input.fail("`\"`")
            if b == 0x5c
                val w = out match
                    Some(w) -> w
                    None ->
                        val w = StringWriter()
                        out = Some(w)
                        w
                w.write(input.text.slice(chunk, input.offset))
                input.offset += 1
                w.writeChar(escaped(input)?)
                chunk = input.offset
            else input.offset += 1)

### Escapes

The single-character escapes are a table. `\u` is the interesting one. Four hexadecimal digits name
a UTF-16 code unit, and a character beyond the first 65,536 is written as two of them: a **high
surrogate** in `d800`–`dbff`, then `\u` and a **low surrogate** in `dc00`–`dfff`. Each holds ten
bits of the character's distance past `0x10000`. A surrogate is not a character by itself, so
one out of place is an error. Each error is reported at the escape that is wrong: a lone low
surrogate at its own `\`, a high one followed by anything but `\u` at what follows it, and a second
escape that is not a low surrogate at its `\`.

    // The character the escape after a `\` stands for, read past.
    private escaped(input: Input) -> Option[char]
        if input.atEnd() then return input.fail("escape")
        val c = input.current()
        val simple = c match
            '"' -> Some('"')
            '\\' -> Some('\\')
            '/' -> Some('/')
            'b' -> Some(char(8))
            'f' -> Some(char(12))
            'n' -> Some('\n')
            'r' -> Some('\r')
            't' -> Some('\t')
            _ -> None
        if simple is Some(_)
            input.offset += 1
            return simple
        if c != 'u' then return input.fail("escape")
        val first = input.offset - 1
        input.offset += 1
        val high = hex4(input)?
        if high < 0xd800 || high > 0xdfff then return Some(char(high))
        if high >= 0xdc00
            input.offset = first
            return input.fail("escape of a character or a high surrogate")
        if !input.text.startsWith("\\u", input.offset) then return input.fail("`\\u` and a low surrogate")
        val second = input.offset
        input.offset += 2
        val low = hex4(input)?
        if low < 0xdc00 || low > 0xdfff
            input.offset = second
            return input.fail("escape of a low surrogate")
        Some(char(0x10000 + ((high - 0xd800) << 10) + (low - 0xdc00)))

    // The value of four hexadecimal digits, read past.
    private hex4(input: Input) -> Option[int]
        var value = 0
        for _ in 0..<4
            val d = if input.atEnd() then -1 else hexValue(int(input.text.byteAt(input.offset)))
            if d < 0 then return input.fail("hexadecimal digit")
            value = value * 16 + d
            input.offset += 1
        Some(value)

A hexadecimal digit's value is its distance from `0`, `a` or `A`, plus ten for a letter: `0x57` is
`'a' - 10`, and `0x37` is `'A' - 10`.

    // The value of the hexadecimal digit `b`, or -1.
    private hexValue(b: int) -> int
        if b >= 0x30 && b <= 0x39 then b - 0x30
        elif b >= 0x61 && b <= 0x66 then b - 0x57
        elif b >= 0x41 && b <= 0x46 then b - 0x37
        else -1
